Patch 82 (rejected dmsynth p36) -- wall-clock resume witness.

REJECTED on the RG353VS on 2026-08-27; retain this patch as a negative result
and do not ship its binary. During an externally observed roughly 20-second
deep suspend, the device's CLOCK_REALTIME advanced only 345 ms before the game
resumed. Therefore GetSystemTimeAsFileTime is not a valid immediate resume
witness on this ROCKNIX image. The exact p36 candidate remained on its
four-tick fallback and did not solve the clock-independent recovery problem.

Apply after history/60-dmsynth-resume-recover.patch. Patch 60 proved on the
RG353VS that the same Wine/game PID survives deep suspend and that restarting
the dmsynth DirectSound transport restores gameplay SFX. Its conservative
four-tick position-stall gate can, however, make the first post-resume effects
audibly late by about one second.

The experiment assumed that system wall time advances while the CPU is in deep
suspend. Compare wall time around the existing timing-thread wait. A gap over
two seconds was intended as a resume witness for this port. On the first
post-resume wake, pass the already-live buffer through patch 60's bounded
Stop+Play re-arm immediately instead of waiting for four stalled ticks. Normal
200 ms notification traffic does not take this arm; the original watchdog
remains as fallback for non-suspend stalls.

There is no new thread, system hook, signal, hot-path log or external polling.
A large forward wall-clock correction can cause one harmless conservative
re-arm; backward corrections do not match.

--- a/dlls/dmsynth/synthsink.c
+++ b/dlls/dmsynth/synthsink.c
@@ -715,6 +715,22 @@
 #define SINK_WATCHDOG_MS 250
 #define SINK_WATCHDOG_STALL 4
 
+/* Patch 36: system time advances while the CPU is in deep suspend. Detect that
+ * discontinuity on the timing thread's first post-resume wake and re-arm the
+ * existing buffer immediately, instead of waiting for four stalled ticks. */
+#define SINK_RESUME_GAP_100NS (2 * (ULONGLONG)10000000)
+
+static ULONGLONG sink_wall_time(void)
+{
+    FILETIME file_time;
+    ULARGE_INTEGER value;
+
+    GetSystemTimeAsFileTime(&file_time);
+    value.LowPart = file_time.dwLowDateTime;
+    value.HighPart = file_time.dwHighDateTime;
+    return value.QuadPart;
+}
+
 static DWORD sink_watchdog_timeout(void)
 {
     static int watchdog_ms = -1;
@@ -795,6 +811,7 @@
